---
term: Material actuality
aka:
  - Material arbitration
  - Arbitration by material actuality
section: plurality-and-actuality
order: 20
definition: The actual conditions and consequences that a purpose must meet to be realized, and that settle conflicts between competing ends through continued existence, without judging them morally.
seeAlso:
  - natural-resistance
  - internal-justification
  - natural-charter
  - limit-of-arbitration
---
The thesis uses Hegel's maxim "What is rational is actual, and what is actual is rational," from the preface to the *Philosophy of Right* (1820), in a restricted sense: it applies to how rational purposes become real in the material world. A purpose must meet actual conditions to be realized, and conceiving it doesn't guarantee its material existence. Material actuality sets the conditions and consequences that every pursuit operates against.

The maxim doesn't mean that everything that exists is morally justified, or that whatever prevails is rational by every ethical system. It also doesn't mean that material success proves an end superior. Rationality within an axiomatic system and material actuality are separate considerations. An end can be internally coherent yet impossible to realize. An end can also be realized while it stays incompatible with its opponents' principles. Material actuality establishes what has happened, not an objective judgment of what ought to have happened.

When ends conflict, material actuality arbitrates between them. Several parties can set entirely incompatible absolute ends. Each can have its own irreducible axioms, a coherent internal justification, procedures for adaptation, and conviction that its pursuit is legitimate. None has to give up its foundational principles because another party disputes them. Their conflict is expressed through material action.

The world determines what happens, what lasts, what's destroyed, what succeeds, and what fails. But arbitration by material actuality isn't moral adjudication. A party that prevails has established a material outcome, not necessarily a universal moral truth. An end that fails to realize itself has met material defeat, not necessarily moral invalidity. The world doesn't deliberate over human purposes. It imposes physical conditions and consequences without offering a moral opinion.

Material actuality gives its verdict through continued existence. An end that persists has material reality's assent to its existence. That assent reconciles reality with the end's internal justification: the end is justified within its own axioms, and it also exists. This holds by definition. If an end doesn't persist, nobody holds it anymore, so nothing remains whose operation needs justifying. The assent is material, not moral. It confirms that an end exists, not that it ought to exist.

Persistence serves as an end's license of operation. The license is internally conditional and externally unconstrained. Internally, it holds only while the end adheres to its axioms. An end that contradicts its internal justification is no longer justified, however long it persists. Externally, nothing else conditions it. The license retires the end's duty to answer moral challenges from outside. Challenges and opposition continue, but the end treats them as part of the conditions of its realization: a source of constructive surprise, not of doubt. See [[protective belt]].

The world's consequences can show that a method was ineffective, that a material assumption was wrong, or that an intended outcome was unattainable. By themselves, they don't show that the foundational principles of an end were morally wrong.

The thesis reverses a line that Hegel quotes from Schiller: "World history is the world's court of judgment" (*Philosophy of Right*, 1820, §340). In the thesis, history decides outcomes but passes no moral judgment. That position follows Hume's gap between *is* and *ought* (*A Treatise of Human Nature*, 1739): what happened doesn't establish what ought to have happened.
